// Max's base Passive - see docs/max-vendetta-fire-mastery.md for the full design. When Max takes
// Health damage (or, with the Unbroken Spirit upgrade, Shield damage), the attacker becomes his
// Vendetta target for a limited time; killing that exact target heals Max for a fraction of the
// damage it dealt him during the mark.

// Vendetta's own base tunables - seeded once by VendettaPassiveData.Apply, permanently bumped by at
// most one field each from future upgrades (Settled Score -> HealMultiplier, Blood Debt ->
// MarkDuration), same "upgrades compose onto one shared component" idiom Weapon.qtn's shared ramp
// pool already established. Cold/static - changes at most a few times ever, never per-tick - kept
// separate from RevengeTarget/StoredRevengeDamage below, which change every tick/every qualifying
// hit respectively.
component RevengeConfig
{
    FP HealMultiplier;
    FP MarkDuration;
}

// Unbroken Spirit upgrade - tag only, no fields. Presence means Shield damage from an active
// RevengeMark also accumulates into RevengeMark.StoredDamage and can itself create/refresh a mark,
// not just Health damage (see MaxVendettaSystem.OnShieldDamageApplied). No PassiveUpgradeData grants
// this yet - added now purely so the base Vendetta system compiles against it; wiring the actual
// upgrade is separate, tracked scope (see docs/max-vendetta-fire-mastery.md's implementation order).
component ShieldDamageCountsForRevenge
{
}

// Lives on the ENEMY that damaged a Vendetta holder, not on the holder itself - this is what lets
// Max (or any future Vendetta holder) keep an arbitrary number of enemies marked simultaneously
// instead of a single "current target": ECS already gives every enemy its own component instance for
// free, so there's no fixed cap to author here (see RevengeMarkTimeoutSystem, which ticks every
// instance of this independently). Lazily added the first time this enemy lands a qualifying hit,
// removed the instant the mark is consumed (this enemy is killed by MarkedBy) or expires
// (RemainingDuration reaches 0). MarkedBy switching to a different Vendetta holder (this enemy hit a
// second holder) resets StoredDamage - a new holder's tally starts from zero, matching the old
// single-target replacement rule but scoped per enemy instead of per holder. Also doubles as the
// target-side display source for CharacterUiWidget's "marked" indicator, same pattern every other
// indicator uses (see CharacterUiWidget.UpdateExplodeOnDeath for the closest precedent).
component RevengeMark
{
    EntityRef MarkedBy;
    FP RemainingDuration;
    FP StoredDamage;
}
